00:00:15.221 --> 00:00:22.232
<v NARRATOR>Bramble met Gaze on a soft evening, watching a teller who never looked up.

4
00:00:22.232 --> 00:00:45.083
<v NARRATOR>A young creature was telling a good tale — but the whole time, she stared down at her own feet. She told the entire story to the dirt. And because she never looked up, the listeners drifted. A squirrel's eyes wandered to the stars. A beetle cleaned his shell. The story floated past the circle.

5
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:50.734
<v GAZE>She's telling it to the ground. The ground isn't listening. WE are.

6
00:00:50.734 --> 00:01:11.465
<v NARRATOR>And then the deer-fawn looked up, and looked slowly, right into the eyes of each creature around the fire, one by one. And one by one, each creature sat up. Each one felt, suddenly, that the evening was about them.

7
00:01:11.465 --> 00:01:16.956
<v BRAMBLE>When you looked at me, I felt like you were about to tell ME a secret.

8
00:01:16.956 --> 00:01:32.887
<v GAZE>My name is Gaze. When I tell a story, I look at my listeners. Not the floor. Not the sky. Them. When I look at someone, they feel held. They feel like the story is theirs.

9
00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:52.578
<v GAZE>And there's a second gift. When I look at their faces, they tell me things. A wide eye says: they're hooked, keep going. A yawn says: I've gone too slow, speed up. A scrunched-up face says: they're confused, say it again.

10
00:01:52.578 --> 00:02:13.109
<v NARRATOR>To show it, she began a tale, her warm gaze drifting around the circle, resting on each creature in turn. When she saw a little mouse's eyes go wide at the scary part, she lingered there, letting it land. When she saw a beetle start to fidget, she quickened the pace.

11
00:02:13.109 --> 00:02:20.880
<v NARRATOR>The story bent and flexed to fit the circle — because she was watching them the whole time.

12
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:30.891
<v BRAMBLE>So many of my kids tell their tales to their own feet. They lose the circle without even knowing it. Would you teach them to look up?

13
00:02:30.891 --> 00:02:40.182
<v GAZE>I will. Looking at someone is the kindest part of telling a story. It says: I know you're here, and this is for you.

14
00:02:40.182 --> 00:02:51.233
<v NARRATOR>Bramble thought how it felt to be truly looked at — the way it pulled you right into the moment — and understood why the ground made such a poor audience.

15
00:02:51.233 --> 00:03:01.244
<v GAZE>A told story is a two-way thing. The teller and the circle breathe together. You can only breathe together if you're looking.

16
00:03:01.244 --> 00:03:15.335
<v NARRATOR>And so the tellers of the circle lifted their eyes at last — and found, looking back, a whole ring of faces that had been waiting all along to be seen.

